            <ArticleTitle>Failure of omalizumab in cholinergic urticaria.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>e127-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/j.1365-2230.2009.03748.x</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Cholinergic urticaria is one of the more common physical urticarias. Although it is often fairly mild, severe treatment-resistant disease may occur, with significant associated disability. Omalizumab, a monoclonal IgG anti-IgE antibody licensed for use in severe asthma, has recently been used successfully in several types of urticaria, including in one case of cholinergic urticaria. This paper reports a patient with severe cholinergic urticaria, unresponsive to antihistamines and multiple other treatments, whose disease was also unresponsive to omalizumab.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
